From: Joel Gerber <Jitsusama@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] screencasting: cast a scaled screen at its logical size

A screen's cast was the size of its mode, so a 4K screen at scale 1.5
went to a call as 3840x2160. A call's encoder spends its bitrate and its
time on every pixel, and some clients can't ask for less: Tuple for
Linux leaves its viewer's requested resolution unimplemented
(SetTransmitResolution) and sends what PipeWire gives it, so a 4K screen
at its 10 Mbit/s cap came across late and blurred.

A scaled screen is now cast at its logical size, the one its windows are
laid out in (2560x1440 for that screen), with each element rescaled to
fit, so text stays as legible as on a screen of that size. The cursor's
position is rescaled with it. Each side is rounded down to even for
encoders that sample colour at half size. A screen at scale 1 and window
casts are unchanged.

---
diff --git a/src/screencasting/mod.rs b/src/screencasting/mod.rs
index 85b3180..03589bd 100644
